00:19:46,060 --> 00:19:50,240
And so, you know, we get up in the morning, we say, all right, God, you know, bless my day.

389
00:19:50,240 --> 00:19:52,260
And then we go on, we make our coffee, and we forget about it.

390
00:19:52,260 --> 00:19:55,680
Or like we might be driving on the interstate, heading to work.

391
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:57,100
We're like, all right, God, you know, give me a good day.

392
00:19:57,100 --> 00:20:00,240
And then we start yelling at somebody because they're in the left lane or they're not passing somebody, you know.

393
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:02,260
And we go back to our prayer.

394
00:20:02,260 --> 00:20:04,140
Thank you, Jesus, for your grace.

395
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:05,320
Right?

396
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:07,160
Come on, I'm guilty of that one.

397
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:11,780
But for real, though, how often do we just pray a prayer just to get it over with?

398
00:20:11,780 --> 00:20:12,300
Right?

399
00:20:12,300 --> 00:20:15,740
And we talk, but we do not give time to listen.

400
00:20:15,740 --> 00:20:16,600
We do not listen.

401
00:20:16,600 --> 00:20:17,280
And what is prayer?

402
00:20:17,280 --> 00:20:18,180
Prayer is a conversation.

403
00:20:18,180 --> 00:20:18,880
Right?

404
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:22,380
Imagine talking to, if you've talked to me, you probably know because I'm a talkaholic.

405
00:20:22,380 --> 00:20:26,020
But if you, you know, you talk to someone, all they do is, and they're like, okay, see ya.

406
00:20:26,020 --> 00:20:27,720
Like, that's kind of rude, isn't it?

407
00:20:27,720 --> 00:20:28,580
Right?

408
00:20:28,580 --> 00:20:32,900
And so, I hate to say it, but sometimes we do the same thing to the Lord.

409
00:20:32,900 --> 00:20:34,980
Where we talk and talk and talk.

410
00:20:35,060 --> 00:20:35,840
We do not listen.

411
00:20:35,840 --> 00:20:37,960
We don't give him time to speak to us.

412
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:39,340
And don't get me wrong.

413
00:20:39,340 --> 00:20:40,580
I think it's great that we pray.

414
00:20:40,580 --> 00:20:42,040
It's a really, really great start.

415
00:20:42,040 --> 00:20:43,660
But we've got to take the time to listen.

416
00:20:43,660 --> 00:20:52,140
We've got to make it a practice to intentionally slow down and hear what God has to say and be intentional with prayer.

417
00:20:52,140 --> 00:20:56,180
Not just to tell God what's up, but to listen to what he wants to say.

418
00:20:56,180 --> 00:21:00,600
And the second thing I think we struggle with is something I believe is a little bit deeper.

419
00:21:00,600 --> 00:21:01,480
A little bit deeper.

420
00:21:01,820 --> 00:21:09,160
Because you can take all the time in the world to listen, but you won't hear anything if you don't know who you're listening for.

421
00:21:09,160 --> 00:21:13,560
Let me show you what I mean in our passage earlier.

422
00:21:13,560 --> 00:21:15,000
John 10, 3 and 4.

423
00:21:15,100 --> 00:21:18,220
I'm sorry, for him.

424
00:21:18,220 --> 00:21:19,200
And the sheep, what?

425
00:21:19,200 --> 00:21:22,120
Recognize his voice and come to him.

426
00:21:22,120 --> 00:21:24,000
He calls his own sheep by name and he leads them out.

427
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:29,460
He has, after he's gathered his own flock, he walks ahead of him and they follow him.

428
00:21:29,460 --> 00:21:30,160
Because why?

429
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:31,600
Because they know his voice.

430
00:21:31,600 --> 00:21:32,540
They know his voice.

431
00:21:32,660 --> 00:21:33,280
John 10, 14.

432
00:21:33,280 --> 00:21:34,200
I am the good shepherd.

433
00:21:34,200 --> 00:21:38,440
I know my own sheep and they know me.

434
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:39,380
John 10, 27.

435
00:21:39,380 --> 00:21:42,200
My sheep listen to my voice.

436
00:21:42,200 --> 00:21:44,120
They listen to my voice.

437
00:21:44,120 --> 00:21:46,800
I know them and they follow me.

438
00:21:46,800 --> 00:21:50,940
And you might ask yourself, well, how do the sheep know the shepherd's voice?

439
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:53,140
The answer is because they know the shepherd.

440
00:21:54,200 --> 00:21:55,100
They know the shepherd.

441
00:21:55,100 --> 00:21:59,020
How do we recognize the voice of God?

442
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:00,140
We get to know God.

443
00:22:00,140 --> 00:22:02,420
We get to know what he sounds like.

444
00:22:02,420 --> 00:22:07,820
The more you spend time with Jesus, the more you'll begin to recognize his voice.

445
00:22:07,820 --> 00:22:14,160
Don't expect to be able to recognize the shepherd's voice if you don't take time to know the shepherd.

446
00:22:14,160 --> 00:22:19,540
Don't expect to hear God's voice if the only time you seek him is when you're in trouble.

447
00:22:19,540 --> 00:22:22,100
And you're like, God, I need a miracle to get out of this.

448
00:22:22,960 --> 00:22:24,640
And that's the only time you go to.

449
00:22:24,640 --> 00:22:32,160
Don't expect to hear God's voice on the regular if the only time you seek him is for an hour and a half at church on Sunday, twice a month.

450
00:22:32,160 --> 00:22:33,480
I'm not trying to be mean.

451
00:22:33,480 --> 00:22:34,280
I'm just trying to be real.

452
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:38,960
You know, his presence moves outside of this room.

453
00:22:38,960 --> 00:22:41,100
His voice speaks outside of his room.

454
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:43,460
We've got to get to know him outside of this room.

455
00:22:43,460 --> 00:22:47,320
We've got to get to know him intimately and personally.

456
00:22:47,320 --> 00:22:48,960
That's what he died to do.

457
00:22:49,680 --> 00:22:56,420
And so if you get to know the Savior of your soul, you'll begin to recognize what he sounds like.

458
00:22:56,420 --> 00:23:00,360
But if you don't take the time to know him, you won't know what he sounds like.

459
00:23:00,360 --> 00:23:01,660
You certainly won't know what he sounds like.

460
00:23:01,660 --> 00:23:08,340
It's the same way that parents can be in a room full of screaming babies and be able to pick out which one's theirs.

461
00:23:08,340 --> 00:23:10,820
Because they know that cry.

462
00:23:10,820 --> 00:23:11,720
I did this earlier.

463
00:23:11,720 --> 00:23:12,540
My son was crying.

464
00:23:12,540 --> 00:23:14,120
He was the only baby crying today.

465
00:23:14,780 --> 00:23:16,000
I was like, that's my boy.

466
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,360
It's the same thing.

467
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:21,900
How do parents know the different cries?

468
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:23,700
All babies kind of sound the same, don't they?

469
00:23:23,700 --> 00:23:26,120
But you can decipher which one is mine.

470
00:23:26,120 --> 00:23:28,820
Because you spend time with your baby.

471
00:23:28,820 --> 00:23:33,160
You're up at 245 changing the sheets and changing a blowout diaper.

472
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:34,980
Poop up the back in the hair.

473
00:23:34,980 --> 00:23:36,880
You know that cry.

474
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:38,800
You know that cry because you know that kid.

475
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:45,720
